      "writtenOn": "2025-02-10T20:25:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No, yielding unsafe types is nearly always worse than yielding safe types even if you\u0027re just going to eagerly convert to something raw. Yielding a reference proves to local code that it\u0027s valid to access for its lifetime, say, to log it, while raw pointers toss this info away and require the user to audit code to ensure the pointers remain valid as expected to do so.\n\nIf you never expect these values to be dereferenced, perhaps yield the relevant `u64` that are used instead of `*const u8`/`usize` to avoid implying it\u0027s a valid buffer to read.",
      "parentUuid": "aa5716b4_81ead44b",
